# Greasy Fork release on push to main

Greasy Fork only picks up a new Better Bunpro release when **`package.json` `version` changes** and lands on `main`. A code push with the same `@version` is invisible to users.

## Before every push to `main` that changes `better-bunpro/`

Do all of these in the same commit (or a follow-up release commit) **before** pushing:

1. **Bump version** in `better-bunpro/package.json` (and keep `package-lock.json` in sync). Semver: patch for fixes, minor for new features.
2. **Update the Greasy Fork info page** `better-bunpro/greasyfork-additional-info.md` so it describes the new or changed behaviour users will see.
3. **Rebuild** (`npm run build` in `better-bunpro/`) so `dist/better-bunpro.user.js` and `dist/better-bunpro.meta.js` carry the new `@version` and description.
4. **Update root `README.md`** feature list when a user-visible feature is added or removed.

## Do not

- Push Better Bunpro behaviour changes to `main` with an unchanged version.
- Treat a rebuild alone as a release — without a version bump Greasy Fork will not update.

## Example

```
# after feature work, before push to main:
# package.json: "version": "0.5.0" → "0.6.0"
# greasyfork-additional-info.md: document the new feature
# npm run build
# commit version + info + dist together, then push
```
